//@ run-pass
//@ description: http_get against a hand-rolled loopback HTTP server returns the expected status and body
//
// ORDERING NOTE (deviates from the plan's draft, per its own sanctioned
// fallback clause): a single OS thread cannot interleave "server accepts +
// sends a response" with `http_get`'s own connect+send+recv, because
// `http_get` bundles all three into one blocking call — by the time its
// internal recv() would need a peer to have sent something, control has
// not (and structurally cannot have) returned to this test function to
// drive the server side. Verified by reasoning through TCP's blocking
// semantics: connect() on a loopback socket can complete before accept()
// is ever called (the handshake completes into the listen backlog
// regardless of accept() timing — this is what tests/run-pass/
// net_socket_loopback.sio already relies on for its listen-then-connect-
// then-accept ordering), but recv() strictly cannot return until a peer
// has actually sent bytes, and nothing in a single sequential thread can
// perform that send while also being blocked inside http_get's own recv().
//
// RESOLUTION: use a real OS-level `fork()` (raw syscall 57 via `syscall6`,
// the same escape hatch this whole plan uses for socket/DNS syscalls — see
// stdlib/net/socket.sio's FIRST compiler limitation). This is genuine
// process-level concurrency from the kernel's perspective, not a Sounio
// "thread" (which the language does not have) — the child process runs the
// hand-rolled server (accept, drain the request, send the canned response,
// close) while the parent runs `http_get` end-to-end as an ordinary
// blocking client. `exit_group` (raw syscall 231) terminates the child
// immediately after its server role so it never falls through into the
// parent's assertions/printing.
//
// PORT-RETRY NOTE — `tcp_listen` here is retried across a small range of
// candidate ports (rather than one hardcoded port) because repeated back-
// to-back runs of this same test (e.g. while developing it) can otherwise
// hit a transient `EADDRINUSE`-style bind failure from a prior run's
// connection still sitting in the kernel's TIME_WAIT state on that exact
// port — this stdlib has no `SO_REUSEADDR` equivalent available (`socket.sio`
// never calls `setsockopt`), so the standard portable workaround is to try
// the next port instead of waiting out TIME_WAIT. This also empirically
// masked what first looked like tuple-return corruption (see
// stdlib/net/http_client.sio's TENTH COMPILER LIMITATION note) — some of
// that flakiness traced back to `tcp_listen` genuinely failing on a reused
// port while its paired error code misread as 0, not to memory corruption
// in the destructured value itself. `listener.fd < 0` (the struct field),
// not the paired scalar error code, is what this file checks, consistent
// with the same workaround applied in `http_client.sio`.

use net::socket::*
use net::http_client::*
use str::lib::*

// REAPING NOTE (fix round 1, addressing a task review finding) — the
// original version of this test forked a child to play the loopback server
// role but never reaped it (no `wait4`/`waitpid`), and had no timeout on
// the child's blocking `tcp_accept`/`tcp_recv` calls. If the PARENT ever
// failed or returned early (e.g. its own `tcp_connect`/`tcp_send` call
// failed) while the child was still blocked waiting on a peer that would
// now never arrive, the child became an orphan that still held this
// process's inherited stdout fd open — and the test runner's
// `output=$(...)` command substitution waits for EVERY holder of the pipe's
// write end to close it, including an orphaned grandchild the runner's own
// `timeout` wrapper can no longer see (once the parent has exited, the
// process `timeout` was tracking is gone, but the orphan survives). That is
// a real risk of wedging the ENTIRE test suite, not just this test.
//
// FIX CHOSEN: (a) unconditional reap-and-kill on every parent exit path,
// not (b) a socket-level SO_RCVTIMEO timeout. Reasoning: (b) would require
// this test to reach into `setsockopt` via a fresh raw `syscall6` (not
// currently exercised anywhere in this plan) on BOTH the listener fd
// (for `accept()`'s timeout, supported by Linux via SO_RCVTIMEO on a
// listening socket since 2.6.28) and the accepted connection fd (for
// `recv()`'s timeout), which is more new, untested raw-syscall surface
// than this task should add on top of everything already stacked in this
// file. (a) is strictly simpler and, more importantly, unconditionally
// correct regardless of what the parent's own socket calls do: it does not
// depend on believing a timeout value guessed here will always be "long
// enough" for a slow CI box yet "short enough" to not itself wedge the
// suite. `reap_child` below guards on `pid > 0` before doing anything: a
// negative `pid` here is not "some other child" to be signaled, it is
// `fork()` having FAILED to create a child at all (the pid==0 branch above
// is the only path that ever spawns one) — POSIX `kill(-1, sig)` is a
// BROADCAST to every process the caller may signal, not a targeted no-op,
// so calling it with `fork()`'s raw -1 return would be far worse than the
// original wedge risk this fix addresses (up to and including killing
// unrelated processes on the machine). With the guard in place, only a
// verified positive pid — meaning `fork()` actually created a child — ever
// reaches `kill`/`wait4`; when `fork()` fails there is nothing to reap,
// and the test simply falls through to `http_get` against a listener no
// child will ever accept on, which fails and is reported normally. Once
// past the guard, `reap_child` sends SIGKILL (signal 9, raw syscall 62,
// `kill(pid, SIGKILL)`) to the child unconditionally — harmless (ESRCH) if
// the child has already exited on its own — and then blocks in `wait4`
// (raw syscall 61) to actually reap it, so no zombie or orphan can outlive
// the parent. This is called from every `return` in `main()` after the
// fork (all three: both early-failure returns and the success return) —
// but that guarantee holds only for those three explicit `return` paths.
// If the `Panic` effect actually fires (a real runtime abort, not a
// `return`) anywhere between the fork and one of those three call sites,
// none of them run and the child is never reaped — the original wedge
// risk could reintroduce itself on that path. This is an accepted
// residual risk, not a solved one: it is structurally narrower than the
// original bug because it now requires BOTH a successful fork AND an
// actual panic to manifest, rather than any ordinary parent-side failure,
// and closing it fully would need compiler-level unwind/cleanup support
// this language does not have.
fn reap_child(pid: i64) with IO, Mut, Panic, Div {
    if pid > 0 {
        syscall6(62, pid, 9, 0, 0, 0, 0)     // kill(pid, SIGKILL) -- ok if already exited
        syscall6(61, pid, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)     // wait4(pid, NULL, 0, NULL) -- blocks until reaped
    }
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Panic, Div {
    var server_ip = Buf4096 { data: [0; 4096] }
    server_ip.data[0] = 49   // "1"
    server_ip.data[1] = 50   // "2"
    server_ip.data[2] = 55   // "7"
    server_ip.data[3] = 46   // "."
    server_ip.data[4] = 48   // "0"
    server_ip.data[5] = 46   // "."
    server_ip.data[6] = 48   // "0"
    server_ip.data[7] = 46   // "."
    server_ip.data[8] = 49   // "1"

    var port: u16 = 18090 as u16
    var listener = TcpSocket { fd: -1 }
    var attempt = 0
    while attempt < 10 && listener.fd < 0 {
        let (listener_raw, listen_err) = tcp_listen(&server_ip, 9, port, 1)
        listener = TcpSocket { fd: listener_raw.fd }
        if listener.fd < 0 {
            port = port + (1 as u16)
        }
        attempt = attempt + 1
    }
    if listener.fd < 0 {
        println("FAIL: tcp_listen returned error on every candidate port")
        return 1
    }

    let pid = syscall6(57, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)   // fork()

    if pid == 0 {
        // CHILD: hand-rolled minimal HTTP server. Accepts TWO connections in
        // sequence (not one) so the parent can drive `http_get` twice against
        // the same server: once with a numeric-IP host (exercising the
        // `is_numeric_ip == true` branch, as before) and once with the
        // hostname "localhost" (exercising the `is_numeric_ip == false` /
        // `resolve_ipv4`/`HostnameBuf` branch, which previously had zero
        // runtime coverage — every existing test only ever called `http_get`
        // with a numeric IP). Each connection: drain (ignore) the request,
        // write back the same fixed response, close.
        var conn_count = 0
        while conn_count < 2 {
            let (conn_raw, _accept_err) = tcp_accept(&listener)
            let conn = TcpSocket { fd: conn_raw.fd }
            if conn.fd >= 0 {
                var drain_buf = Buf4096 { data: [0; 4096] }
                let (conn2_raw, _drained) = tcp_recv(conn, &!drain_buf)
                let conn2 = TcpSocket { fd: conn2_raw.fd }

                // "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Length: 5\r\n\r\nhello" (43 bytes,
                // computed byte-by-byte, not copied from the plan's guess).
                let resp: [i32; 43] = [
                    72, 84, 84, 80, 47, 49, 46, 49, 32, 50, 48, 48, 32, 79, 75, 13, 10,
                    67, 111, 110, 116, 101, 110, 116, 45, 76, 101, 110, 103, 116, 104, 58, 32, 53, 13, 10, 13, 10,
                    104, 101, 108, 108, 111
                ]
                var response_buf = Buf4096 { data: [0; 4096] }
                var i = 0
                while i < 43 {
                    response_buf.data[i as usize] = resp[i as usize] as u8
                    i = i + 1
                }
                let (conn3_raw, _sent) = tcp_send(conn2, &response_buf, 43)
                let conn3 = TcpSocket { fd: conn3_raw.fd }
                tcp_close(conn3)
            }
            conn_count = conn_count + 1
        }
        tcp_close(listener)
        syscall6(231, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)   // exit_group(0) — never returns
        return 0
    }

    // PARENT: ordinary client via http_get. The listener fd isn't needed
    // here (only the child accepts on it) — close this process's copy.
    tcp_close(listener)

    var host = HttpPathBuf { data: [0; 256] }
    host.data[0] = 49
    host.data[1] = 50
    host.data[2] = 55
    host.data[3] = 46
    host.data[4] = 48
    host.data[5] = 46
    host.data[6] = 48
    host.data[7] = 46
    host.data[8] = 49

    var path = HttpPathBuf { data: [0; 256] }
    path.data[0] = 47   // "/"

    let expected_body = str_from_literal("hello")

    // FIRST CALL — numeric IP host (the branch every other net_ test already
    // exercises via `is_numeric_ip == true`).
    let response = http_get(&host, 9, port, &path, 1)
    if response.ok != true {
        println("FAIL: http_get did not succeed (numeric-IP host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }
    if response.status != 200 {
        println("FAIL: expected status 200 (numeric-IP host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }
    if !str_eq(&response.body, &expected_body) {
        println("FAIL: response.body did not equal \"hello\" (numeric-IP host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }

    // SECOND CALL — hostname host ("localhost", not a numeric IP). This
    // exercises `is_numeric_ip == false` and the `resolve_ipv4`/`HostnameBuf`
    // path inside `http_get`, which previously had zero runtime coverage
    // (every existing net_ test only ever passed a numeric IP as the host).
    // `tests/run-pass/net_dns_resolve.sio` already proves `resolve_ipv4`
    // itself resolves "localhost" correctly in isolation; this exercises the
    // same resolution end-to-end through `http_get`.
    var hostname_host = HttpPathBuf { data: [0; 256] }
    hostname_host.data[0] = 108  // 'l'
    hostname_host.data[1] = 111  // 'o'
    hostname_host.data[2] = 99   // 'c'
    hostname_host.data[3] = 97   // 'a'
    hostname_host.data[4] = 108  // 'l'
    hostname_host.data[5] = 104  // 'h'
    hostname_host.data[6] = 111  // 'o'
    hostname_host.data[7] = 115  // 's'
    hostname_host.data[8] = 116  // 't'

    let response2 = http_get(&hostname_host, 9, port, &path, 1)
    if response2.ok != true {
        println("FAIL: http_get did not succeed (hostname host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }
    if response2.status != 200 {
        println("FAIL: expected status 200 (hostname host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }
    if !str_eq(&response2.body, &expected_body) {
        println("FAIL: response.body did not equal \"hello\" (hostname host)")
        reap_child(pid)
        return 1
    }

    println("PASS: http_get round-trip matched (numeric IP + hostname)")
    reap_child(pid)
    return 0
}
